\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Dispersion curves of the coupled wave. Red and blue curves show symmetric and anti-symmetric modes of uncoupled waves, respectively. The black dots represent the dispersion of the coupled wave. The direction of the external magnetic field is (a) $\bm  {H}_0\parallel \bm  {k} (\varphi =0)$, (b) $\varphi =\pi /4$, and (c) $\bm  {H}_0\perp \bm  {k}\ (\varphi =\pi /2)$. The insets are magnifications of some of the band crossings. Level repulsion occurs at crossings of same colored curves i.e. between modes with the same parity.}}{5}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:Bands}{{2}{5}{Dispersion curves of the coupled wave. Red and blue curves show symmetric and anti-symmetric modes of uncoupled waves, respectively. The black dots represent the dispersion of the coupled wave. The direction of the external magnetic field is (a) $\bm {H}_0\parallel \bm {k} (\varphi =0)$, (b) $\varphi =\pi /4$, and (c) $\bm {H}_0\perp \bm {k}\ (\varphi =\pi /2)$. The insets are magnifications of some of the band crossings. Level repulsion occurs at crossings of same colored curves i.e. between modes with the same parity}{figure.2}{}}
